// ─── Where the hints come from (a sketch of std and us in v4) ───────────────
//
// Nothing in the journal names a tax line, an expense account or a basis.
// Every `▸` in this example follows from a declaration like these. The
// editor shows which declaration a hint came from, so the reasoning is
// always one click away.
//
// How a flow gets its purpose, first match wins:
//   1. written on the flow or leg:   `#repair of condo`
//   2. its contract:                 `contract flat with greystar  … #rent`
//   3. its party's kind:             `kind grocer … purpose groceries`
//   4. its money's kind:             `kind fund … pays dividend`
//   5. its accounts' kinds:          `kind 401k … takes pre-tax-deferral from wages`
// Two sources that disagree are an error naming both. When none says, the
// flow is `unclassified`. A "description" still says why in words, and
// `check --strict` asks for a purpose. Declarations use `:` for "is a kind
// of", and flows use `#` for a purpose, so the two never meet.

// ─── Purposes: what money is for (std) ──────────────────────────────────────

purpose spending
purpose food : spending
purpose groceries : food
purpose dining : food
purpose home : spending
purpose rent : home
purpose phone : home
purpose household : home
purpose fun : spending
purpose insurance : spending
purpose premium : insurance

/// Money spent to make a thing last longer, work better or do something new.
/// It is not spent: it joins the thing named by `of`, and leaves when the
/// thing is sold, or bit by bit as it depreciates.
purpose improvement : capital
  of asset                          // a purpose may require an object, typed

/// Money spent to keep a thing as it was: spent now.
purpose repair : spending
  of asset

purpose income
purpose wages : income
purpose dividend : income
purpose interest : income
purpose tax-paid                    // what reached a tax authority

// ─── Parties: what dealing with each kind means (std) ───────────────────────

kind grocer : merchant
  purpose groceries                 // what paying one is for
kind landlord : org
  purpose rent
kind phone-company : merchant
  purpose phone
kind employer : org
  purpose wages                     // what money from one is
kind tax-authority : government
  purpose tax-paid                  // for the year of the flow that pays it
kind store : merchant
  has sales-tax percent             // derived out of every price paid to one

// ─── Money: what a kind of commodity does (std) ─────────────────────────────

kind currency : commodity
  select fifo
kind fund : commodity
  pays dividend                     // `VTI -> fidelity 198.12 USD` is a dividend

// ─── Allocations: shares of a cost (std) ────────────────────────────────────
//
// `business 60% for studio`, on a contract, a party or a purpose, means that
// 60% of each flow is a cost of `studio`. It is derived, per flow, as a flow
// of its own purpose: `studio`'s share of the phone. The whole bill stays
// the phone. `flow` shows both, and the tax laws count the share.

// ─── Tax meanings (us) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

purpose wages
  law count-wages
    count amount as wages

purpose tax-paid
  law count-payments
    when to is irs
    count amount as payments        // for the year the paying flow is for

kind sole-proprietorship : entity
  /// A cost allocated to the business, or paid by it, is deductible on
  /// Schedule C.
  law business-costs
    on cost                         // a flow the business bears, or its share of one
    count amount as schedule-c-expenses

// ─── Assets that wear out (us/rental) ───────────────────────────────────────

kind rental-home : property
  has in-service date
  has land amount
  /// Residential rental property is depreciated straight-line over 27.5
  /// years from the month it is placed in service (mid-month), on its cost
  /// less the land. An improvement starts its own 27.5 years.
  ///
  /// To fix: set `in-service` to the day the home was first available to rent.
  law depreciation
    each month
    depreciate (self.cost - self.land) over 27.5y from self.in-service mid-month
                                    // an effect: consumes basis, counts `depreciation`

  law rental-income
    on in
    when purpose is rent of self
    count amount as rental-income

  law rental-expenses
    on out
    when purpose is repair of self | insurance of self | interest of self
    count amount as rental-expenses
